{%- comment -%}
  Product card — one template, rendered once per product.

  **This is a single block, not one per product**, exactly as
  `_collection-card` is. The section holds the collection and renders this
  static block inside its own loop, handing each pass the piece it is for:

      {% content_for 'block', type: '_product-card', id: 'product-card',
                     closest.product: product %}

  So eight pieces show eight cards from one set of settings, and the theme
  editor lists it once with the repeat mark. Anything that would have to differ
  per card therefore has no place here; it comes from the product itself.

  Its name is underscored because it is **private**: a merchant never adds it,
  the section places it. Only its children are in an "Add block" list.

  **The children are placed by CSS, not by their order in the tree**, again as
  the collection card's are. `.card--composed` names one grid row `media`, so
  the photograph and the quick-view disc claim it whatever order they are
  dragged into and every other child flows underneath in the order the merchant
  chose.

  The card-wide product link is structural markup here. Product names are
  ordinary Text blocks connected to `closest.product.title`; the link must not
  require a second Product Title block just to make the card clickable.

  **`snippets/product-card.liquid` is untouched and stays the card everywhere
  else.** The collection and search templates still render it, so the theme
  carries one card built two ways for as long as those templates are what they
  are. The class names and every `data-card-*` hook are identical between them
  — that is the contract `theme.js` binds to, and it reads them all from the
  `[data-card]` root rather than by walking the tree, which is what lets the
  markup be rearranged like this at all.

  **Everything under Theme settings → Product cards that is still a *setting*
  lives on these blocks now**, so a merchant edits the card where the card is:
  the frame, the fit, the framing correction and the spin on the photograph
  block, the button's words on the button block, the alignment and the quick
  view here. The theme settings stay because the snippet card still reads them,
  and they are **not** fallbacks — nothing here consults them, and changing one
  moves the collection and search grids while leaving this card alone. That is
  the price of one card built two ways, and it ends when those templates are
  rebuilt.

  Everything else that was a setting is now the presence of a block, which is
  what a block is for: the caption, the option control, the price, the "in
  stock" note, the button.

  **`data-card-multi` is the one thing the root states for a child**, because
  the CSS selector that drives the no-JavaScript hover swap is
  `.card[data-card-multi]` — it has to be here. It is gated on the photograph
  count rather than the slide count, for the reason recorded in CLAUDE.md, and
  it costs nothing when the image block is removed, there being no slides for
  it to act on.

  **The quick view is a setting rather than a block, and per device.** It is
  the one control here that is not about arranging anything: the disc is a
  fixed corner of the photograph, and whether it is worth showing is a question
  about the pointer rather than about composition — a hover affordance on a
  phone is a tap target competing with the card's own link. Two checkboxes, so
  a shop can keep it on a desktop and drop it on everything narrower.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign product = closest.product
  assign card_photos = product.media | where: 'media_type', 'image'

  assign quick_class = ''
  unless block.settings.quick_view_desktop
    assign quick_class = quick_class | append: ' card__quick--hide-wide'
  endunless
  unless block.settings.quick_view_compact
    assign quick_class = quick_class | append: ' card__quick--hide-compact'
  endunless

  assign quick_view_on = false
  if block.settings.quick_view_desktop or block.settings.quick_view_compact
    assign quick_view_on = true
  endif

  assign quick_view_label = 'products.quick_view_of' | t: title: product.title

  comment
    **The views are the card's, not the photograph block's**, and moving them
    here cost one rearrangement worth understanding. A block cannot read its
    parent's settings, so the image block cannot be *told* whether to draw the
    arrows or count the video — but the card can state the answer on its own
    root, where the stylesheet and `theme.js` both read it. The image block goes
    on rendering the slides and the arrows; what changes is who decides they are
    shown.

    The video is the exception that had to move rather than be gated: its chip
    carries the merchant's word, which is a setting here, and the image block
    would have no way to receive it. `.card__spin` is absolutely positioned at
    the top of the media box, and `.card--composed` pins the photograph to the
    `media` grid row — so the card can draw it in that same row, exactly as it
    already draws the quick-view disc.
  endcomment
  assign video_label = block.settings.video_badge

  comment
    The chip is gated on the chain it depends on — there is no video view
    without the carousel and no chip without the video — and on having something
    to say. **Clearing the words is how it is turned off**, rather than a
    checkbox beside a text field that would only ever mean the same thing. The
    editor hides each control the same way, so a setting that could not act is
    never offered.
  endcomment
  assign card_videos = product.media | where: 'media_type', 'video'
  assign card_has_video = false
  if block.settings.show_carousel and block.settings.show_video and block.settings.video_badge != blank and card_videos.first != blank
    assign card_mp4 = card_videos.first.sources | where: 'format', 'mp4'
    if card_mp4.last != blank
      assign card_has_video = true
    endif
  endif
-%}

<div
  class="card card--composed"
  data-card
  {% if card_photos.size > 1 %}data-card-multi{% endif %}
  {% unless block.settings.show_carousel %}data-card-carousel="off"{% endunless %}
  data-card-arrows-wide="{{ block.settings.arrows_wide }}"
  data-card-arrows-compact="{{ block.settings.arrows_compact }}"
  {% unless block.settings.hover_next %}data-card-hover="off"{% endunless %}
  {% unless block.settings.show_video %}data-card-video="off"{% endunless %}
  style="--card-align: {{ block.settings.text_align }};"
  {{ block.shopify_attributes }}
>
  {% content_for 'blocks' %}

  {%- if product != blank -%}
    <a class="card__link" href="{{ product.url }}" aria-label="{{ product.title | escape }}"></a>
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    A real link to the piece, which `theme.js` upgrades into the overlay.
    Without scripting it lands on the product page: the same information, one
    navigation further away — so turning both checkboxes off removes a
    convenience and never a route.

    It is the card's own markup rather than a block because it is not part of
    the flow the merchant arranges: it sits in the `media` grid row, over the
    photograph, and there is nowhere else in a card for it to go.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if quick_view_on and product != blank -%}
    <a
      class="card__quick{{ quick_class }}"
      href="{{ product.url }}"
      data-quick-view
      data-tip="{{ 'products.quick_view' | t }}"
      aria-label="{{ quick_view_label }}"
    >
      {% render 'icon', icon: 'eye' %}
    </a>
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    The chip shown while the video is the view on screen. `theme.js` unhides it
    when it steps to that slide, and it starts hidden wherever the card opens on
    a photograph. It carries the merchant's word rather than the design's
    "360°", which is true of that footage and of nothing else.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if card_has_video -%}
    <span class="card__spin" data-card-spin-label{% if card_photos.size > 0 %} hidden{% endif %}>
      {{- video_label -}}
    </span>
  {%- endif -%}
</div>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "t:blocks.product_card.name",
  "tag": null,
  "blocks": [
    { "type": "_product-card-media" },
    { "type": "_product-card-option-values" },
    { "type": "_product-card-option-control" },
    { "type": "text" },
    { "type": "rich-text" },
    { "type": "button" },
    { "type": "icon" },
    { "type": "border" },
    { "type": "_product-card-price" },
    { "type": "_product-card-add" },
    { "type": "_product-card-group" }
  ],
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "text_align",
      "label": "t:blocks.product_card.text_align.label",
      "options": [
        { "value": "left", "label": "t:settings_schema.options.left" },
        { "value": "center", "label": "t:settings_schema.options.center" }
      ],
      "default": "left"
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "t:blocks.product_card.views_header"
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "show_carousel",
      "label": "t:blocks.product_card.show_carousel.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.product_card.show_carousel.info",
      "default": true
    },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "arrows_wide",
      "label": "t:blocks.product_card.arrows_wide.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.product_card.arrows_wide.info",
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.show_carousel }}",
      "options": [
        { "value": "always", "label": "t:blocks.product_card.arrows.always" },
        { "value": "hover", "label": "t:blocks.product_card.arrows.hover" },
        { "value": "never", "label": "t:blocks.product_card.arrows.never" }
      ],
      "default": "hover"
    },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "arrows_compact",
      "label": "t:blocks.product_card.arrows_compact.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.product_card.arrows_compact.info",
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.show_carousel }}",
      "options": [
        { "value": "always", "label": "t:blocks.product_card.arrows.always" },
        { "value": "swipe", "label": "t:blocks.product_card.arrows.swipe" },
        { "value": "never", "label": "t:blocks.product_card.arrows.never" }
      ],
      "default": "swipe"
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "hover_next",
      "label": "t:blocks.product_card.hover_next.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.product_card.hover_next.info",
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.show_carousel }}",
      "default": true
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "show_video",
      "label": "t:blocks.product_card.show_video.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.product_card.show_video.info",
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.show_carousel }}",
      "default": true
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "video_badge",
      "label": "t:blocks.product_card.video_badge.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.product_card.video_badge.info",
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.show_carousel and block.settings.show_video }}",
      "default": "Video"
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "t:blocks.product_card.quick_view_header"
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "quick_view_desktop",
      "label": "t:blocks.product_card.quick_view_desktop.label",
      "default": true
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "quick_view_compact",
      "label": "t:blocks.product_card.quick_view_compact.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.product_card.quick_view_compact.info",
      "default": true
    }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}
